## Goal
Show how the six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) cluster, using a principal component analysis of the gene-level counts, with each sample coloured by its condition.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib). No inputs are modified.

1. Read the `counts_gene` matrix (genes x samples; `gene_id` and `gene_name` are annotation columns, the six sample columns are the data) and the `design` table; check that the sample columns match the design's `sample_id` values exactly.
2. Transform: `log2(count + 1)` on the length-scaled counts as given. Drop genes with zero counts in every sample; no other filtering.
3. Select genes: the 500 most variable genes across samples (variance of the log2 values; all genes if fewer than 500 remain).
4. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` (centred, no scaling of genes, `svd_solver="full"`) on the samples x genes matrix, computing all components (at most 5 for six samples).
5. Plot PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by `condition` from the design, points labelled with `sample_id`, axes labelled with percent variance explained.
6. Write the PC coordinates per sample (with condition) and the variance explained per component.

## Outputs
Types come from the closed vocabulary in _references/artifact_types.md.

